locking hierarchy:
	lower hierarchy should be locked earlier, unlocked later.
	higher hierarchy should be locked later, unlocked earlier.
	same hierarchy shouldn't be locked at once.

	This lists only the major mutexes.  See doc/internal/ja/gfmd-internal.txt
	(the mutex section) for details, including the dead_file_copy mutexes,
	and the "LOCKS:" comments in the source code.
	Note that some "LOCKS:" comments still use obsolete names such as
	host::back_channel_mutex or host::channel_mutex, which mean
	abstract_host::mutex now.

	level	mutex
	  1	giant_mutex (giant_lock())
	  2	peer_table_mutex
	  3	dfc_allq.mutex, host_busyq.mutex
	  4	dbq.mutex, journal file mutex
	  4	removal_pendingq.mutex, removal_finishedq.mutex
	  4	abstract_host::mutex
	  5	dfc->mutex, callout_module.mutex, and other leaf mutexes

	i.e.
	locking order must be:
		giant -> peer_table -> dbq
			peer_free() calls db_begin()/db_end() while holding
			peer_table_mutex.
		giant -> journal file mutex
			if giant_lock is needed while holding the journal file
			mutex, unlock the journal file mutex first, then lock
			giant and the journal file mutex in this order.
		giant -> dfc_allq -> (dfc OR abstract_host)
		giant -> host_busyq -> removal_pendingq -> dfc
		giant -> host_busyq -> abstract_host
		dfc_workq (removal_pendingq, removal_finishedq, host_busyq) -> dfc
		giant -> abstract_host
	unlocking order must be the reverse.
	dfc_allq.mutex and dbq.mutex shouldn't be locked at once.

	leaf mutexes, which must not be held while locking another mutex:
		callout_module.mutex, dfc->mutex, watcher_event::mutex,
		peer_closing_queue.mutex, total_disk_mutex,
		dead_file_copy_scan.mutex
